        "math_academy_id": "q-83193",
        "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)$ about the point $x=1.$",
        "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x) \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20611=0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =(1)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-{x}^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =-(1)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}=2{x}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(1) & =2(1)^{-3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x) & =0+(1)(x-1)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}+\u22ef \\\\  & =(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
